  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic empowerment</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNDP economic empowerment programme workshops were conducted in Fiji, Solomon Islands and Vanuatu in which 2130 women from rural and, peri-urban and urban centres were identified and underwent the training. This has resulted in a marked increase in women participation in small and micro-enterprises in the three countries and upskilling on business and digital literacy such as mobile or internet banking</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Economic policy reform and management and private sector development</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNDP launched a Blue Accelerator Grant Scheme in collaboration with Fiji Drua Incubator, indirectly benefited approximately 30,000 individuals living in coastal Fijian communities who are vulnerable to climate change impacts. The sustainable aquaculture initiative to benefit over 500 community-based aquaculture farms in Fiji, providing reliable, healthy brood stock. UNDP incubated eight highly transformative projects in Fiji, supported 18 young entrepreneurs in Vanuatu to develop Social Impact Business models, including developing e-commerce platforms and projects that are aligned to the acceleration of SDGs, cognisant of the need to ensure long-term viability of both green and blue economies of the Pacific.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">UNDP through Australia&#x2019;s funding support co-invested in the National ID system for Vanuatu which reached the peak of its results in 2022 and early 2023 with 93% of the population in Vanuatu now having legal identity with a unique citizen identifier. 302,012 citizens, which is 93% of total population of 324,000, were registered and have a legal identity by birth certificate and 88% of the adult population above 18 have National ID Cards issued. Males (94,170) and Females (94,281) .This support has resulted in leveraging synergies between the different identity registries and generation of major operational and cost-savings.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2023-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
